+
+## Experimental MicroPython validation branch
+
+This branch moves browser database/configuration imports behind `BrowserData`
+BFF methods, uses portable async iterators for streamed responses, and uses the
+framework's shared browser builder. It requires pytincture's
+`feat/browser-runtime-choice` branch and the latest wapyt voice branch plus the
+runtime-validation fixes (asset hashes, browser task scheduling, output capture,
+clipboard handling, and theme persistence).
+
+With those framework/widget versions installed in the build environment:
+
+```sh
+python -m pytincture.browser_build --config pyproject.toml
+python tests/runtime_server.py
+python tests/browser_runtime_smoke.py --runtime micropython
+python tests/browser_runtime_smoke.py --runtime pyodide
+python tests/browser_voice_smoke.py --runtime micropython
+python tests/browser_voice_smoke.py --runtime pyodide
+```
+
+Install Playwright and Chromium for the browser tests. The voice test uses the
+committed synthetic speech fixture and real faster-whisper `base.en`, whose
+first run downloads the model. No human microphone interaction is needed.
+
+The local server listens at `http://127.0.0.1:8094/chat?runtime=micropython` and
+uses the public demo login `demo@example.com` / `demo-password`. It sets
+`allow_microphone=True`, uses a disposable database under the user's cache
+folder, and supplies a local OpenAI-compatible fixture through the real
+LiteLLM/BFF stack. Do not use this fixture launcher for production.
+
+Provider metadata and transcript persistence use real local database operations;
+transcripts are scoped to the authenticated user's email-derived identifier.
+User-access cards remain the app's in-memory demonstration UI; they are not an
+authorization system. Chat deletion clears local widget conversations; it does
+not purge the backend transcript archive. Live provider credentials, identity
+providers, and physical microphone permissions are outside these local checks.
